  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://portal.mardi4nfdi.de/entity/Q6821334">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://portal.mardi4nfdi.de/entity/Q68663"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Frumkin isotherm model</rdfs:label>
    <schema:description xml:lang="en">adsorption isotherm with lateral adsorbate interactions</schema:description>
    <mathmoddb:P286 rdf:resource="https://portal.mardi4nfdi.de/entity/Q6775858"/>
    <mathmoddb:P286 rdf:resource="https://portal.mardi4nfdi.de/entity/Q6821332"/>
    <mathmoddb:P147 rdf:resource="https://portal.mardi4nfdi.de/entity/Q6821333"/>
    <mathmoddb:P1560 rdf:resource="https://portal.mardi4nfdi.de/entity/Q6821331"/>
    <mathmoddb:P1495 rdf:resource="https://portal.mardi4nfdi.de/entity/Q6534265"/>
    <mathmoddb:P1460 rdf:resource="https://portal.mardi4nfdi.de/entity/Q6534270"/>
    <mathmoddb:P1684 rdf:resource="https://portal.mardi4nfdi.de/entity/Q6775766"/>
    <mathmoddb:P1459 xml:lang="en">The Frumkin isotherm model is an adsorption model that extends the Langmuir framework by explicitly accounting for lateral interactions between adsorbed molecules. It assumes monolayer adsorption on a surface with energetically equivalent sites while allowing attractive or repulsive interactions among neighboring adsorbates. The strength and sign of these interactions influence the shape of the adsorption isotherm and the variation of adsorption energy with surface coverage. In the absence of lateral interactions, the Frumkin model reduces to the Langmuir isotherm. The model is closely related to the Fowler–Guggenheim isotherm and is widely applied in solution-phase adsorption studies, including chemical wastewater treatment.</mathmoddb:P1459>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://portal.mardi4nfdi.de/entity/Q6775840">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://portal.mardi4nfdi.de/entity/Q68663"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Emax model</rdfs:label>
    <schema:description xml:lang="en">non-cooperative hyperbolic dose–response model</schema:description>
    <mathmoddb:P147 rdf:resource="https://portal.mardi4nfdi.de/entity/Q6775842"/>
    <mathmoddb:P1560 rdf:resource="https://portal.mardi4nfdi.de/entity/Q6775841"/>
    <mathmoddb:P286 rdf:resource="https://portal.mardi4nfdi.de/entity/Q6775861"/>
    <mathmoddb:P1495 rdf:resource="https://portal.mardi4nfdi.de/entity/Q6534265"/>
    <mathmoddb:P1460 rdf:resource="https://portal.mardi4nfdi.de/entity/Q6534270"/>
    <mathmoddb:P1459 xml:lang="en">The Emax model in its non-cooperative form is a pharmacodynamic dose–response model that describes how drug effect increases with concentration in a simple hyperbolic manner. It assumes no cooperativity in the system, meaning the Hill coefficient is fixed at one, producing a non-sigmoidal saturation curve. The model is characterized by a maximal achievable effect and the concentration that produces half of this maximum, offering intuitive measures of drug efficacy and potency. As the n = 1 specialization of the Hill dose-response model, it represents the simplest form of saturable concentration–effect behavior. This non-cooperative Emax model is widely used in pharmacology, toxicology, and pharmacokinetic–pharmacodynamic analyses when dose–response relationships do not exhibit sigmoidal characteristics.</mathmoddb:P1459>
  </rdf:Description>
