  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://portal.mardi4nfdi.de/entity/Q6775654">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://portal.mardi4nfdi.de/entity/Q68663"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Andrews model</rdfs:label>
    <schema:description xml:lang="en">microbial growth model accounting for substrate inhibition</schema:description>
    <mathmoddb:P1495 rdf:resource="https://portal.mardi4nfdi.de/entity/Q6534265"/>
    <mathmoddb:P1460 rdf:resource="https://portal.mardi4nfdi.de/entity/Q6534270"/>
    <mathmoddb:P1684 rdf:resource="https://portal.mardi4nfdi.de/entity/Q6775644"/>
    <mathmoddb:P1459 xml:lang="en">The Andrews model is a mathematical model that describes the specific growth rate of micoorganisms. It modifies the traditional Monod model to account for the negative effects of high substrate concentrations. When the substrate level becomes too high, the specific growth rate decreases due to inhibition. This model is widely used in fermentation and other bioprocesses to predict growth under inhibitory conditions.</mathmoddb:P1459>
    <mathmoddb:P147 rdf:resource="https://portal.mardi4nfdi.de/entity/Q6775655"/>
    <mathmoddb:P1560 rdf:resource="https://portal.mardi4nfdi.de/entity/Q6775658"/>
    <mathmoddb:P286 rdf:resource="https://portal.mardi4nfdi.de/entity/Q6775657"/>
    <mathmoddb:P558 rdf:resource="https://portal.mardi4nfdi.de/entity/Q6775765"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://portal.mardi4nfdi.de/entity/Q6820962">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://portal.mardi4nfdi.de/entity/Q68663"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Fowler-Guggenheim isotherm model</rdfs:label>
    <schema:description xml:lang="en">mathematical model for adsorption with lateral interactions</schema:description>
    <mathmoddb:P558 rdf:resource="https://portal.mardi4nfdi.de/entity/Q561111"/>
    <mathmoddb:P558 rdf:resource="https://portal.mardi4nfdi.de/entity/Q2595344"/>
    <mathmoddb:P286 rdf:resource="https://portal.mardi4nfdi.de/entity/Q6775858"/>
    <mathmoddb:P147 rdf:resource="https://portal.mardi4nfdi.de/entity/Q6820963"/>
    <mathmoddb:P147 rdf:resource="https://portal.mardi4nfdi.de/entity/Q6820964"/>
    <mathmoddb:P1560 rdf:resource="https://portal.mardi4nfdi.de/entity/Q6820968"/>
    <mathmoddb:P1495 rdf:resource="https://portal.mardi4nfdi.de/entity/Q6534265"/>
    <mathmoddb:P1460 rdf:resource="https://portal.mardi4nfdi.de/entity/Q6534270"/>
    <mathmoddb:P1684 rdf:resource="https://portal.mardi4nfdi.de/entity/Q6775766"/>
    <mathmoddb:P1459 xml:lang="en">The Fowler–Guggenheim isotherm model is an adsorption model that incorporates lateral interactions between adsorbed molecules. Unlike Langmuir’s assumption of independent adsorption sites, this model allows the heat of adsorption to vary with surface coverage. Attractive interactions raise adsorption energy with increasing coverage, while repulsive interactions cause the opposite trend. The model provides a more realistic description for adsorbates that influence each other after adsorption. It is commonly applied in surface chemistry and environmental adsorption analysis, including chemical wastewater treatment.</mathmoddb:P1459>
  </rdf:Description>
